      "questionVault": [
        {
          "question": "Explain why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964\u201368. (12 marks)",
          "stimulus1": "Gulf of Tonkin incident (1964)",
          "stimulus2": "The Tet Offensive (1968)",
          "clue": "Explain how the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution acted as a blank check, how containment and domino theory drove troop increases, and how the Tet Offensive shocked military leaders into asking for more troops.",
          "answer": "\u201COne reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964\u201368 was the political authorization granted by the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution in August 1964.\u201D After alleged clashes between the destroyer [[USS Maddox]] and North Vietnamese patrol boats, Congress passed the resolution giving President Johnson a 'blank check' to deploy combat troops without a formal declaration of war. {{Therefore, the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution was a vital cause of why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964\u201368 because it removed legislative barriers, allowing the deployment of Marines to Da Nang in March 1965.}}\n\n\u201CA second reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964\u201368 was the strategic commitment to the Cold War policy of containment and the Domino Theory.\u201D US military planners believed that if South Vietnam fell to communism, neighboring nations like Laos and Cambodia would fall next. To prevent this, General Westmoreland implemented [[search-and-destroy]] missions, raising troop levels to [[535,000]] by 1968. {{Therefore, the geopolitical policy of containment was a fundamental reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964\u201368 because it made stopping communism in South Vietnam a top national security priority.}}\n\n\u201CA third reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964\u201368 was the strategic crisis and loss of control caused by the Tet Offensive in early 1968.\u201D The Vietcong launched a massive surprise attack on over 100 cities, including the US Embassy in Saigon. Though militarily defeated, the [[Tet Offensive]] shattered US claims of imminent victory and led to requests for [[206,000]] more troops. {{Therefore, the military shock of the Tet Offensive was a decisive reason why US military involvement in Vietnam escalated in the years 1964\u201368 because it proved current force levels were failing, forcing a choice between withdrawal or further escalation.}}"
        }
      ],
      "summaryCorrection": {
        "text": "In August 1964, the Gulf of Tonkin incident occurred. The US destroyer Maddox claimed it was attacked by North Vietnamese patrol boats. In response, Congress passed the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution, which served as a 'blank check' for President Johnson. Johnson then launched Operation [[Desert Storm -> Rolling Thunder]], a massive bombing campaign, and sent the first combat troops to Da Nang in March [[1968 -> 1965]]. Defensive historians argue that Johnson escalated the war to defend [[communism -> South Vietnamese sovereignty]] against unprovoked aggression. However, revisionist historians argue that the incident was [[entirely true and accurate -> exaggerated or fabricated]] by the administration to justify a pre-planned military expansion, locking the US into an unwinnable ground war."
      },
