      "questionVault": [
        {
          "question": "Explain why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969\u201372. (12 marks)",
          "stimulus1": "President Nixon's 'Silent Majority' speech",
          "stimulus2": "Fear of communist expansion",
          "clue": "Focus on working-class patriotism and fear of communism, Nixon's political appeals to the Silent Majority, and the class resentment toward draft-exempt student protesters.",
          "answer": "\u201COne reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969\u201372 was their deep-seated patriotism and fear of communist expansion.\u201D Many conservative and working-class citizens believed that a communist victory in South Vietnam would damage US global credibility. They accepted the [[Domino Theory]] and argued that abandoning an ally would lead to a communist bloodbath. {{Therefore, anti-communist patriotism was a key reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969\u201372 because they viewed the conflict as a necessary stand against global communism.}}\n\n\u201CA second reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969\u201372 was President Nixon's successful political mobilization of the Silent Majority.\u201D In his November 1969 speech, Nixon called on the quiet, law-abiding majority of Americans to support his policy of [[Vietnamisation]]. This speech gave a voice to patriotic citizens, presenting them as the moral backbone of the country against radicals. {{Therefore, Nixon's political appeal to the Silent Majority was a crucial reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969\u201372 because it framed support for his policies as a patriotic duty.}}\n\n\u201CA third reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969\u201372 was class resentment against middle-class student anti-war protesters.\u201D Working-class families, whose drafted sons did not have college deferments, saw student demonstrators as privileged, unpatriotic draft-dodgers who insulted the military. This division led to pro-war events like the [[Hard Hat Riots]] of 1970. {{Therefore, working-class resentment of student protesters was a major reason why some Americans supported US involvement in the Vietnam War in the years 1969\u201372 because it aligned patriotic sentiment against the anti-war movement.}}"
        }
      ],
